Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 05:16:16 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 139/143] render: a scenetexture set to an image file's path
 loads that image

A Wallpaper Engine preset ships the images its scenetexture slots use in its own folder (`files/<name>.png`,
`.gif`, ...), as plain image files: Wallpaper Engine keeps a picked image as a path, never as a .tex inside the scene.
The core resolved every texture name as `materials/<name>.tex` through the scene's containers, which refuse a path
outside them and read only WE's TEX format, so the host had no way to put such an image in its slot.

RenderContext::resolveTexture now takes a name that is an absolute path ending in .png, .jpg, .jpeg, .bmp, .tga or
.gif as an image file: it is read and checked with stbi_info_from_memory, then wrapped as the texture a .tex holding
a PNG / JPEG is (TEXB0003 with a FreeImage format: one image, one level of the file's bytes), so it decodes to RGBA8
on the decode pool like those (a GIF's first frame), keeps its own size (no power-of-two padding: an image object's
UVs stay whole) and is clamped at the edges as WE's image textures are. The shader-format pass sees a free image (RGBA8,
format 0). A file that can't be read or isn't an image throws AssetLoadException, which every caller already logs as a
missing asset and survives by keeping the slot's texture. Any other name resolves as before.

liascape's host sets the property to the preset's image (resolved and confined to the preset's folder by its
backend) before the renderer build; textures bind at the build, so a live change doesn't rebind the slot.

Tested by liascape's host_api_test usertexture_loads_an_image_file (a generated PNG in the slot; a missing file and
a file that isn't an image keep the slot's texture).

Origin: ours
